216	<h3><span class="num">Rule 12 &middot; SKILL.md, “The curator's role and decisions”, a new bullet after the bullet on a worker's decision &middot; new today, in place of version 5's redraft &middot; waits for your read</span>A review weighs each finding</h3>
217	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text: a new bullet, every word new</span>
218	<ul><li>A worker asked for issues returns its findings at one weight, so your own judgement sorts them by what each needs before any reaches the curator or becomes the next brief. Only a finding that would change a decision of the curator's becomes an ask. A reason that is no longer true is cut, since bringing the record in line with reality needs no approval. A correction of an entry's ground that changes no decision goes to the entry's history, since it changes nothing that a reader does. A finding that confirms a decision takes one line.</li></ul>
219	<p class="where-in">The bullet before it, as main has it</p>
220	<ul><li>If a decision that a worker wrote in one line of its report is consequential, it is not made until the curator sees it. Bring it by one of the three ways.</li></ul>
221	</div></div>
222	<details class="prior"><summary>Old text: none on main; version 5's redraft, in “The standing goal”, which this text replaces (tap to open)</summary><div>
223	<ul><li><span class="cut">A worker's result becomes the brief of the next step only after your own judgement, against the goal and the record. A worker answers what it was asked, to the letter, and returns trivial points beside those that matter.</span></li></ul>
224	</div></details>
225	<div class="note"><p><strong>What it changes and why.</strong> Today a review's findings reach you, or the next brief, as the worker returned them, all at one weight. With this bullet, the coordinator sorts them first, and only a change of your decision comes to you, as an ask. A reason that the tree made false is cut, a correction of an entry's ground goes to its history, and a confirmation is one line. Each of those three follows a rule of yours that the skill does not state today: your words of 31 October on bringing the record in line with reality, and of 29 October on corrections of grounding. The bullet keeps version 5's point, your own judgement before a result becomes the next brief. It moves beside the search rule and the rule on a worker's decisions, since all three govern what reaches you.</p></div>
226	<p><strong>Your words.</strong></p>
227	<blockquote class="held"><p>“Where was the problem then? Were the instructions in the skill contradicting? Like, what do I need to correct to not have such a frustrating experience again?”</p>
228	<p>“… you don't need my approval to bring the record in line with reality.”</p>
229	<p>“… it sounds like something that should go into POSITIONS' history entry to correct the historical grounding, not [?] the wording of the position itself. I cannot imagine what I would do differently based on the added segment.”</p>
230	<p>“… if I ask a worker to find issues in a text, it will give me consequential as well as trivial points, and this judgment part I don't see it here in the new changed wording emphasized enough.”</p></blockquote>
231	<p><strong>The answer.</strong> The judgement step is now the sort itself, with what each kind of finding needs, so that only your decisions reach you. It stays the coordinator's own step, and no worker makes it in its place.</p>
