108	<p class="eyebrow">Fortress revival &middot; the revival page &middot; 3 November 2026</p>
109	<h1>The revival page: two versions, the drift, and the hybrid</h1>
110	<p class="standfirst">A version is one publish of the page &ldquo;the revival so far&rdquo;. The text is the Markdown file in the repository with the same content. Sizes are in characters of the text.</p>
111	</header>
112	
113	<section id="v20" class="part">
114	<h2><span class="num">Part 1</span>Version 20, the text she received</h2>
115	<p>Version 20 was published on 13 October. Its text is 31.6K. Her letter links this text, and git holds it. Its page, 43.5K, isn&rsquo;t on disk.</p>
116	<ul>
117	<li>The header and the glossary, 1.8K. The glossary defines seven words, such as the switch-over: the point at which the compiled path moves onto the interpreter&rsquo;s library.</li>
118	<li>Part 1, the exploring era to 16 September, 3.2K: the old tree built again, the microGPT ports, and the first 309 rows of the gap ledger.</li>
119	<li>Part 2, the batches to 10 October, 7.6K. A batch is one run of the workflow that changes the tree. The part gives 13 decisions, and two numbers by batch through batch 13b. The checker count is the errors that the checker reports on the library. The distance is all its errors, with no early stop. The part ends with where the tokens went.</li>
120	<li>Part 3, how the record grew, 2.3K: a chart and eight lines on it.</li>
121	<li>Part 4, the skills and the process since 4 October, 2.7K.</li>
122	<li>Part 5, what the revival changed in Fortress for a programmer, 3.5K.</li>
123	<li>Part 6, the original Fortress and the revival&rsquo;s resolutions, 10.6K. It opens with &ldquo;Custodians, not authors&rdquo;, and resolves 12 contradictions between the team&rsquo;s sources.</li>
124	</ul>
125	<div class="sources"><span class="label">References</span><ol class="refs">
126	<li><code>revival-story.md</code> at <code>9556daf62</code></li>
127	<li><code>revival-page-drift.md</code>, section 3</li>
128	</ol></div>
129	</section>
