74	<h2><span class="num">1 of 6</span>The two post-mortems</h2>
75	<p>A post-mortem is a review of what went wrong. An ask is a message that puts one decision to you.</p>
76	<h3>The Haiku post-mortem, 19 October</h3>
77	<p>On 16 October your phone app switched the coordinator to Haiku, the smallest model tier, with one of your messages. It ran on Haiku for 683 replies. You saw it on 19 October at 21:04 (23:04 yours) and wrote: &ldquo;This is a disaster. I never intentionally switched to Haiku&rdquo;.</p>
78	<p>It ruled that the code of climb batches 17 and 18, two runs of work, stands, and that the coordinator's messages to you were at fault, among them a wrong ask on the integer power. Four lessons landed that night in the skills, the files of instructions that Claude loads. To land is to commit to the main branch. Among them are the model check, an alarm when the coordinator isn't on Opus or Fable, and the rule that a line on the held list, where the coordinator keeps your points, is never your yes.</p>
79	<ul>
80	<li>It had ten asks. You answered asks 1 to 5 on 19 and 20 October, and four of them became entries of POSITIONS, the file of your decisions, one entry each.</li>
81	<li>You didn't answer asks 6 to 10. They ask who decides changes to the coordinator skill, the skill for how the coordinator works, two library questions, a page for each landing, and a push notification that names its batch. A page is a web page that the coordinator publishes for you. The library questions wait in the review queue, the list of decisions for your review, at their defaults. A default is the option that stands until you choose.</li>
82	</ul>
83	<h3>The post-mortem of 28 October</h3>
84	<p>On 26 October the clarity workflow, the tool that rewrites record entries, rewrote 13 entries of POSITIONS. At 19:08 (20:08 yours) you rejected them all: &ldquo;It is in the Claude speak. It is not in the ASD-STE100 register.&rdquo; On 28 October at 07:42 (08:42 yours) you asked: &ldquo;This last one was an utter failure. What went wrong?&rdquo;</p>
85	<p>It ruled that the coordinator's brief, its instructions to the run, had overridden the tool's own instructions, and that a worker's report was taken as the plan, with no judgement between them.</p>
86	<ul>
87	<li>It had five asks. You chose the tool, and the pilot, a first small run that you read: at 21:12 (22:12 yours), &ldquo;a pilot should be 3-4 entries, not 9&rdquo;. You said yes to the quiz, a test with made-up cases.</li>
88	<li>The findings that touch your decisions became questions. Only P-146 still waits.</li>
89	<li>You didn't answer the fifth ask, three proposals for the skills. Your reply at 23:07 (00:07 yours) didn't choose, and the proposals sit on a branch that was never merged.</li>
90	</ul>
